::CW_U05_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Author presentation context: Which opening is strongest?{
=A. "After the Rain" is a short prose piece meant to show a character who is safe but still unsure how to move forward.#Correct. This names title, form, and purpose. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. My story has a driveway, rain, and an umbrella, and then things happen.#Incorrect. This mostly summarizes events without purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I do not know what my piece is about.#Incorrect. The author needs to frame the work clearly.
~D. The audience should figure out everything with no help.#Incorrect. An author presentation should provide a useful entry point.
}

::CW_U05_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which sentence best explains craft reasoning?{
~A. I used an umbrella because umbrellas exist.#Incorrect. This does not explain craft purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The story has words and sentences.#Incorrect. This is too general. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I made choices because writing needs choices.#Incorrect. This does not explain a specific reader effect.
=D. I repeated the umbrella image so the reader could see the character holding onto protection even after the rain stopped.#Correct. This names the craft choice and explains reader effect.
}

::CW_U05_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which author presentation mistake should be corrected?{
~A. Naming the purpose of the creative work.#Incorrect. Naming purpose is useful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Retelling every event so the audience has no reason to read the piece.#Correct. The presentation should guide the audience, not replace the creative work.
~C. Explaining a revision decision.#Incorrect. Revision reflection strengthens the presentation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Asking the audience to notice a repeated image.#Incorrect. This can prepare readers effectively. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which audience frame is strongest?{
~A. Do not pay attention to anything.#Incorrect. This does not guide the audience. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I will explain every symbol before you read.#Incorrect. This overexplains and may replace the creative experience.
=C. As you read, notice how the repeated umbrella image changes from protection to hesitation.#Correct. This gives the audience a specific craft feature to notice.
~D. The piece is about stuff.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which revision reflection is strongest?{
=A. I removed the sentence that named the feeling so the final action could show uncertainty instead of explaining it directly.#Correct. This names the change and explains why the final version is stronger.
~B. I changed it because revision was required.#Incorrect. This does not explain improvement. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I changed random sentences for no reason.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I did not revise anything because first drafts are always final.#Incorrect. The course expects purposeful revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}
